# Environment variable template (Ch. 21 §21.6, Ch. 23 §23.3)
#
# RULES
# - Placeholders only. Never commit real credentials, tokens, or keys.
# - Every value here must also appear in 07-ops/01-deployment/environment-config.md with
#   its purpose, example value, and security note.
# - Values marked SECRET must never be printed in logs or error messages.

# =============================================================================
# THIS PROJECT HAS NO ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES. THE FILE IS INTENTIONALLY EMPTY.
# =============================================================================
#
# Not "none yet" — none, by design, and the reasons are constraints rather than
# preferences:
#
#   CON-001  The kit ships as a Claude Code plugin: Markdown files, no runtime,
#            no process. Nothing exists to read an environment variable.
#   CON-003  No network calls at run time. There is no endpoint to point at.
#   CON-006  No paid services and no API keys. There is no credential to supply.
#   ADR-002  The kit ships no executable code. FF-009 fails the build if any
#            appears in the published payload.
#
# Consequently:
#   - The kit holds no secret of its own. Nothing to configure, rotate, or leak.
#   - There is no APP_ENV, because there is no local/test/production distinction
#     for a folder of Markdown installed on a developer's machine.
#   - There is no LOG_LEVEL, because there is no log (CON-007, BR-014).
#   - There is no feature flag, because a flag is a branch, and the Simplicity
#     driver's measure counts branches (FF-001). Intake depth is the single
#     argument, passed on the command, not configured here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IF YOU ARE ABOUT TO ADD A LINE TO THIS FILE, STOP.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A variable here means the kit has gained a runtime, a network call, a secret,
# or a configurable branch. Each of those supersedes an accepted ADR. Write the
# superseding ADR first; then this file follows from it.
#
# See: 07-ops/01-deployment/environment-config.md
#      01-docs/07-security-and-reliability/security-specification.md  §5
